Let $p=[t^3, t]^{T}$. 
Then the continuous $\mathrm{VMPUM}$ is the same as the discrete $\mathrm{VMPUM}$, that is, 
equal to $\{ c [t^3, t]^{T} \mid c\in K\}$. Direct computation over $\mathcal{S}_1$ yields 
$$ \ker_{\mathcal{S}_1}(\kappa_p) = {}_{\mathcal{S}_1}\langle [0, \Delta^2], \; [0, t\Delta-1], \;  [1, -t^2] \rangle$$
and this means that 
$$
\left[ 
\begin{array}{cc}
0 & \Delta^2 \\ 0 & t\Delta-1 \\ 1 & -t^2
\end{array}
\right] 
$$
is a kernel representation of the $\mathrm{VMPUM}$ of $p$. 
Note that over $\mathcal{A}_1$,
we have
$$ \ker_{\mathcal{A}_1}(\kappa_p) = {}_{\mathcal{A}_1}\langle [0, \partial^2], \; [0, t\partial -1], \;  [1, -t^2] \rangle.$$



Alternatively, we can compute $ \ker_{\mathcal{S}_1}(\kappa_p)$ in the commutative framework, using the analogue of ``difference algebra'' approach. At first, we observe that
$$ 
\mathrm{Shift}_{ [t^3, t]^{T}}= {}_{K[t]} \langle t^3, 3t^2+3t+1, 6t+6, 6, t, 1 \rangle
$$
